window.SICP_EX4 = [

  // ── Section 4.1: The Metacircular Evaluator ─────────────────────────
  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.1', html: '<p>Notice that we cannot tell whether the metacircular evaluator evaluates operands from left to right or from right to left. Its evaluation order is inherited from the underlying Lisp. Write a version of <code>list-of-values</code> that evaluates operands from left to right regardless of the order of evaluation in the underlying Lisp. Also write a version that evaluates from right to left.</p>', code: '; Left to right:\n; (define (list-of-values exps env)\n;   (if (no-operands? exps) \'()\n;       (let ((first (eval (first-operand exps) env)))\n;         (cons first (list-of-values (rest-operands exps) env)))))\n;\n; Right to left:\n; (define (list-of-values exps env)\n;   (if (no-operands? exps) \'()\n;       (let ((rest (list-of-values (rest-operands exps) env)))\n;         (cons (eval (first-operand exps) env) rest))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.2', html: '<p>Louis Reasoner plans to reorder the <code>cond</code> clauses in <code>eval</code> so that the clause for procedure applications appears before the clause for assignments.</p><p>a. What happens if he does this? Give an example of a problematic expression.</p><p>b. Louis is unphased. He proposes using <code>(call factorial 3)</code> syntax for applications. Change <code>eval</code> to support this.</p>', code: '; a. (define x 3) would be treated as an application:\n;    try to apply the value of "define" to args x and 3.\n;\n; b. (define (application? exp) (tagged-list? exp \'call))\n;    (define (operator exp) (cadr exp))\n;    (define (operands exp) (cddr exp))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.3', html: '<p>Rewrite <code>eval</code> so that the dispatch is done in data-directed style. Compare this with the data-directed differentiation procedure of exercise 2.73.</p>', code: '; (define (eval exp env)\n;   (cond ((self-evaluating? exp) exp)\n;         ((variable? exp) (lookup-variable-value exp env))\n;         (else\n;          (let ((proc (get \'eval (car exp))))\n;            (if proc\n;                (proc exp env)\n;                (apply (eval (operator exp) env)\n;                       (list-of-values (operands exp) env)))))))\n;\n; (put \'eval \'quote (lambda (exp env) (text-of-quotation exp)))\n; (put \'eval \'set! eval-assignment)\n; (put \'eval \'define eval-definition)\n; ...' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.4', html: '<p>Recall the definitions of the special forms <code>and</code> and <code>or</code> from chapter 1. Implement <code>and</code> and <code>or</code> as new special forms for the evaluator, and as derived expressions in terms of <code>if</code>.</p>', code: '; As special forms:\n; (define (eval-and exps env)\n;   (cond ((null? exps) #t)\n;         ((null? (cdr exps)) (eval (car exps) env))\n;         ((eval (car exps) env) (eval-and (cdr exps) env))\n;         (else #f)))\n;\n; As derived expressions:\n; (and a b c) => (if a (if b c #f) #f)\n; (or a b c) => (let ((v a)) (if v v (or b c)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.5', html: '<p>Scheme allows an additional syntax for <code>cond</code> clauses, <code>(⟨test⟩ =&gt; ⟨recipient⟩)</code>. If ⟨test⟩ evaluates to a true value, then ⟨recipient⟩ is evaluated and applied to the test value. Modify the handling of <code>cond</code> so that it supports this extended syntax.</p>', code: '; Check if a cond clause has => syntax:\n; (define (cond-recipient-clause? clause)\n;   (eq? (cadr clause) \'=>))\n; (define (cond-recipient clause) (caddr clause))\n;\n; In expand-clauses:\n; (if (cond-recipient-clause? first)\n;     (list (list \'lambda (list \'v)\n;             (list \'if \'v (list (cond-recipient first) \'v)\n;                   (expand-clauses rest)))\n;           (cond-predicate first))\n;     ... normal clause ...)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.6', html: '<p>Implement <code>let</code> as a derived expression by transforming it into a <code>lambda</code> application. Show the transformation and install it in <code>eval</code>.</p>', code: '; (let ((x 1) (y 2)) (+ x y))\n; => ((lambda (x y) (+ x y)) 1 2)\n;\n; (define (let? exp) (tagged-list? exp \'let))\n; (define (let-bindings exp) (cadr exp))\n; (define (let-body exp) (cddr exp))\n; (define (let->combination exp)\n;   (cons (make-lambda (map car (let-bindings exp))\n;                      (let-body exp))\n;         (map cadr (let-bindings exp))))\n;\n; Add to eval: ((let? exp) (eval (let->combination exp) env))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.7', html: '<p>Implement <code>let*</code> as a derived expression, in terms of nested <code>let</code> expressions.</p>', code: '; (let* ((x 3) (y (+ x 1))) (* x y))\n; => (let ((x 3)) (let ((y (+ x 1))) (* x y)))\n;\n; (define (let*->nested-lets exp)\n;   (define (expand bindings body)\n;     (if (null? bindings) (cons \'begin body)\n;         (list \'let (list (car bindings))\n;               (expand (cdr bindings) body))))\n;   (expand (cadr exp) (cddr exp)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.8', html: '<p>&ldquo;Named <code>let</code>&rdquo; allows: <code>(let fib-iter ((a 1) (b 0) (count n)) ...)</code>. Modify <code>let->combination</code> to support named let by transforming it into a local <code>define</code> and call.</p>', code: '; (let name ((v1 e1) (v2 e2)) body)\n; =>\n; (begin (define (name v1 v2) body)\n;        (name e1 e2))\n;\n; Or equivalently, using letrec or internal define.\n; Check if (cadr exp) is a symbol to detect named let.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.9', html: '<p>Many languages include a variety of iteration constructs, such as <code>do</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, and <code>until</code>. In Scheme, iterative processes can be expressed in terms of ordinary procedure calls, so special iteration constructs provide no essential gain in computational power. On the other hand, such constructs are often convenient. Design some iteration constructs, give examples of their use, and show how to implement them as derived expressions.</p>', code: '; Example: while loop\n; (while condition body ...)\n; =>\n; (define (loop) (if condition (begin body ... (loop))))\n; (loop)\n;\n; Example: for loop\n; (for (i 0 10) body ...)\n; =>\n; (let loop ((i 0))\n;   (if (< i 10) (begin body ... (loop (+ i 1)))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.10', html: '<p>Using data abstraction, we were able to write an <code>eval</code> procedure that is independent of the particular syntax of the language to be evaluated. Invent an alternative syntax for some form (for example, <code>let</code>) and modify the appropriate syntax procedures to support it.</p>', code: '; Example: Python-like let syntax\n; (bind x = 1, y = 2 in (+ x y))\n;\n; Just change the selectors:\n; (define (let-bindings exp) ...parse new syntax...)\n; (define (let-body exp) ...parse new syntax...)\n;\n; The evaluator (eval, let->combination) stays the same.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.11', html: '<p>Instead of representing a frame as a pair of lists (variables and values), we can represent a frame as a list of bindings, where each binding is a name-value pair. Rewrite the environment operations to use this alternative representation.</p>', code: '; (define (make-frame variables values)\n;   (map cons variables values))\n; (define (frame-variables frame) (map car frame))\n; (define (frame-values frame) (map cdr frame))\n; (define (add-binding-to-frame! var val frame)\n;   (set-cdr! frame (cons (car frame) (cdr frame)))\n;   (set-car! frame (cons var val)))\n;\n; lookup-variable-value scans bindings:\n; (define (lookup-variable-value var env)\n;   (define (scan bindings)\n;     (cond ((null? bindings) (env-loop (enclosing-environment env)))\n;           ((eq? var (caar bindings)) (cdar bindings))\n;           (else (scan (cdr bindings)))))\n;   ...)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.12', html: '<p>The procedures <code>set-variable-value!</code>, <code>define-variable!</code>, and <code>lookup-variable-value</code> can be described in terms of more abstract procedures for traversing the environment structure. Define abstractions that capture the common patterns and redefine the three procedures in terms of those abstractions.</p>', code: '; (define (scan-frame var frame if-found if-not-found)\n;   (define (scan vars vals)\n;     (cond ((null? vars) (if-not-found))\n;           ((eq? var (car vars)) (if-found vals))\n;           (else (scan (cdr vars) (cdr vals)))))\n;   (scan (frame-variables frame) (frame-values frame)))\n;\n; (define (env-scan var env if-found if-not-in-env)\n;   (if (eq? env the-empty-environment) (if-not-in-env)\n;       (scan-frame var (first-frame env)\n;         if-found\n;         (lambda () (env-scan var (enclosing-environment env)\n;                             if-found if-not-in-env)))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.13', html: '<p>Scheme allows us to create new bindings for variables by means of <code>define</code>, but provides no way to get rid of bindings. Implement for the evaluator a special form <code>make-unbound!</code> that removes the binding of a given variable from the environment in which the <code>make-unbound!</code> expression is evaluated. Should <code>make-unbound!</code> remove the binding only from the first frame of the environment?</p>', code: '; (define (make-unbound! var env)\n;   (let ((frame (first-frame env)))\n;     (define (scan vars vals prev-vars prev-vals)\n;       (cond ((null? vars) \'ok)  ; not found\n;             ((eq? var (car vars))\n;              (if prev-vars\n;                  (begin (set-cdr! prev-vars (cdr vars))\n;                         (set-cdr! prev-vals (cdr vals)))\n;                  (begin (set-car! frame (cdr vars))\n;                         (set-cdr! frame (cdr vals)))))\n;             (else (scan (cdr vars) (cdr vals) vars vals))))\n;     (scan (frame-variables frame) (frame-values frame) #f #f)))\n;\n; Only remove from first frame — safer, respects scope.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.14', html: '<p>Eva Lu Ator and Louis Reasoner each try to install the primitive <code>map</code> as a procedure available inside the metacircular evaluator. Eva installs the underlying Scheme <code>map</code> as a primitive; Louis installs a version written in the metacircular evaluator. Only Eva&rsquo;s works. Explain.</p>', code: '; Louis\'s map calls a procedure argument using the\n; underlying Scheme apply, but the procedure is a\n; metacircular evaluator compound procedure — it\'s a\n; list structure (\'procedure params body env), not a\n; Scheme closure. The underlying Scheme can\'t call it.\n;\n; Eva\'s primitive map receives the underlying Scheme\n; version, which expects Scheme closures — also wrong.\n;\n; Solution: implement map inside the evaluator.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.15', html: '<p>Given a one-argument procedure <code>p</code> and an object <code>a</code>, <code>p</code> is said to &ldquo;halt&rdquo; on <code>a</code> if evaluating the expression <code>(p a)</code> returns a value. Show that it is impossible to write a procedure <code>halts?</code> that correctly determines whether <code>p</code> halts on <code>a</code> for any procedure <code>p</code> and object <code>a</code>.</p>', code: '; Classic halting problem proof by contradiction:\n; Suppose halts? exists. Define:\n;\n; (define (run-forever) (run-forever))\n; (define (try p)\n;   (if (halts? p p) (run-forever) \'halted))\n;\n; Consider (try try):\n; If (halts? try try) => runs forever. Contradiction.\n; If not (halts? try try) => halts. Contradiction.\n; Therefore halts? cannot exist.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.16', html: '<p>In this exercise we implement the method of scanning out internal definitions described in the text.</p><p>a. Change <code>lookup-variable-value</code> to signal an error if the value it finds is the symbol <code>*unassigned*</code>.</p><p>b. Write a procedure <code>scan-out-defines</code> that transforms a procedure body containing internal definitions into one with <code>let</code> and <code>set!</code>.</p><p>c. Install <code>scan-out-defines</code> in <code>make-procedure</code> or <code>procedure-body</code>. Which is better?</p>', code: '; b. Transform:\n; (lambda (x) (define u e1) (define v e2) body)\n; into:\n; (lambda (x)\n;   (let ((u \'*unassigned*) (v \'*unassigned*))\n;     (set! u e1) (set! v e2) body))\n;\n; Install in make-procedure (scans once at creation time)\n; rather than procedure-body (would scan on every call).' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.17', html: '<p>Draw diagrams of the environment in effect when evaluating the body of a procedure with internal definitions, comparing the simultaneous-scope and sequential-scope approaches. Why is there an extra frame in the transformed program? Explain why this difference in environment structure can never make a difference in the behavior of a correct program.</p>', code: '; The let creates an extra frame for u and v.\n; Original: defines go directly in the procedure\'s frame.\n; Transformed: let creates a frame inside the procedure frame.\n;\n; Correct programs don\'t reference u or v before they\'re\n; assigned, so the extra frame is invisible to them.\n; Only incorrect programs (using *unassigned* values)\n; would behave differently.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.18', html: '<p>Consider an alternative strategy for scanning out definitions that translates the example into:</p><pre>(lambda ⟨vars⟩\n  (let ((u \'*unassigned*) (v \'*unassigned*))\n    (let ((a ⟨e1⟩) (b ⟨e2⟩))\n      (set! u a) (set! v b))\n    ⟨e3⟩))</pre><p>Will this work if the internal definitions are for mutually recursive procedures? Will it work for the <code>solve</code> procedure from section 3.5.4?</p>', code: '; This doesn\'t work for the solve procedure.\n; (let ((a e1) (b e2)) ...) evaluates e1 and e2 in an\n; environment where u and v are *unassigned*.\n; For solve, the streams y and dy reference each other\n; through delayed evaluation — but the let evaluates\n; their init expressions before the set! assignments.\n;\n; Mutually recursive procedures work because lambda\n; captures the environment but doesn\'t evaluate the body\n; until called (after the set! assignments).' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.19', html: '<p>Ben, Alyssa, and Eva disagree about what the result should be of evaluating:</p><pre>(let ((a 1))\n  (define (f x) (define b (+ a x)) (define a 5) (+ a b))\n  (f 10))</pre><p>Ben says the result should follow sequential scoping (a=1 for b, then a=5). Alyssa says it should signal an error (a is *unassigned* when b is computed). Eva says it should be 20 (simultaneous scope, a=5 for both). Who is right?</p>', code: '; Eva\'s answer (simultaneous scope, a=5) is most\n; consistent with the idea that internal defines are\n; all in scope simultaneously.\n;\n; But implementing Eva\'s semantics requires solving\n; a system of equations, which is difficult in general.\n; Alyssa\'s approach (error on premature reference)\n; is practical and catches bugs.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.20', html: '<p>Because internal definitions look sequential but are really simultaneous, some people prefer to use <code>letrec</code> instead. Implement <code>letrec</code> as a derived expression by transforming it to a <code>let</code> with assignments.</p>', code: '; (letrec ((f (lambda (x) ...)) (g (lambda (y) ...))) body)\n; =>\n; (let ((f \'*unassigned*) (g \'*unassigned*))\n;   (set! f (lambda (x) ...))\n;   (set! g (lambda (y) ...))\n;   body)\n;\n; This is the same transformation as scan-out-defines.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.21', html: '<p>Amazingly, Louis&rsquo;s intuition in exercise 4.20 is correct. It <em>is</em> indeed possible to specify recursive procedures without using <code>letrec</code> (or even <code>define</code>), although the method for accomplishing this is much more subtle. The following expression computes 10 factorial by applying a recursive factorial procedure (the Y combinator technique):</p><pre>((lambda (n)\n   ((lambda (fact) (fact fact n))\n    (lambda (ft k) (if (= k 1) 1 (* k (ft ft (- k 1)))))))\n 10)</pre><p>Verify that this works. Then show how to do mutual recursion (even? and odd?) using the same trick.</p>', code: '((lambda (n)\n   ((lambda (fact) (fact fact n))\n    (lambda (ft k) (if (= k 1) 1 (* k (ft ft (- k 1)))))))\n 10)\n\n; Mutual recursion:\n((lambda (x)\n   ((lambda (even? odd?)\n      (even? even? odd? x))\n    (lambda (ev? od? n)\n      (if (= n 0) #t (od? ev? od? (- n 1))))\n    (lambda (ev? od? n)\n      (if (= n 0) #f (ev? ev? od? (- n 1))))))\n 11)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.22', html: '<p>Extend the evaluator in this section to support the special form <code>let</code>. (This is easy to do since we have already defined <code>let->combination</code> in exercise 4.6.)</p>', code: '; In the analyzing evaluator, add:\n; ((let? exp) (analyze (let->combination exp)))\n;\n; let->combination transforms the let into a lambda\n; application, which analyze then handles normally.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.23', html: '<p>Alyssa P. Hacker doesn&rsquo;t understand why <code>analyze-sequence</code> needs to be so complicated. She proposes a simpler version. Eva Lu Ator explains that Alyssa&rsquo;s version doesn&rsquo;t pick the right thing for a body with a single expression, or for a body with two expressions. Explain.</p>', code: '; Alyssa\'s version:\n; (define (analyze-sequence exps)\n;   (define (execute-sequence procs env)\n;     (cond ((null? (cdr procs)) ((car procs) env))\n;           (else ((car procs) env)\n;                 (execute-sequence (cdr procs) env))))\n;   (let ((procs (map analyze exps)))\n;     (lambda (env) (execute-sequence procs env))))\n;\n; This works, but doesn\'t optimize at analysis time.\n; The original loops through procs at analysis time,\n; building a chain of lambda calls.\n; Alyssa\'s loops at execution time — less efficient.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.24', html: '<p>Design and carry out some experiments to compare the speed of the original metacircular evaluator with the version in this section that separates analysis from execution.</p>', code: '; Test with a recursive procedure like fibonacci:\n;\n; (define (fib n)\n;   (if (< n 2) n (+ (fib (- n 1)) (fib (- n 2)))))\n;\n; (fib 25) ; time both evaluators\n;\n; The analyzing evaluator should be faster because\n; it analyzes the fib body once, not on every call.\n; Speedup is most visible for deeply recursive procedures.' },

  // ── Section 4.2: Lazy Evaluation ────────────────────────────────────
  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.25', html: '<p>Suppose that (in ordinary applicative-order Scheme) we define <code>unless</code> as</p><pre>(define (unless condition usual-value exceptional-value)\n  (if condition exceptional-value usual-value))</pre><p>Can you use <code>unless</code> in applicative order? What about normal order?</p>', code: '; In applicative order, both usual-value and\n; exceptional-value are evaluated before unless runs.\n; This defeats the purpose if one branch has a side\n; effect or error.\n;\n; In normal order (lazy evaluation), arguments are\n; not evaluated until needed, so unless works correctly:\n; only the selected branch is evaluated.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.26', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle and Alyssa P. Hacker disagree over whether <code>unless</code> should be a special form or a procedure. Ben wants it as a special form; Alyssa argues it should be a procedure so it can be used with higher-order procedures like <code>map</code>. Give an example where Alyssa&rsquo;s approach is useful, and show how to implement <code>unless</code> as a derived expression.</p>', code: '; Alyssa\'s use case:\n; (map unless conditions usual-vals exceptional-vals)\n;\n; Can\'t pass a special form to map — only procedures.\n;\n; As derived expression:\n; (unless cond usual except)\n; => (if cond except usual)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.27', html: '<p>Suppose we type the following definitions to the lazy evaluator:</p><pre>(define count 0)\n(define (id x) (set! count (+ count 1)) x)\n(define w (id (id 10)))</pre><p>Give the response the lazy evaluator would give when evaluating <code>count</code> and <code>w</code>.</p>', code: '; After (define w (id (id 10))):\n; count = 1 (outer id forced by define, inner id delayed)\n;\n; Typing w:\n; Forces the thunk (id 10), which increments count\n; w = 10, count = 2' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.28', html: '<p><code>eval</code> uses <code>actual-value</code> rather than <code>eval</code> to evaluate the operator of a combination. Give an example that demonstrates the need for this.</p>', code: '; If operator is a variable bound to a thunk:\n; (define (f x) (+ x 1))\n; (define (g h) (h 5))   ; h is lazy => thunk\n; (g f)                  ; must force h to get f\n;\n; If eval is used instead of actual-value for the\n; operator, h would remain a thunk and we\'d try to\n; "apply" a thunk instead of a procedure.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.29', html: '<p>Exhibit a program that you would expect to run much more slowly without memoization than with memoization. Also, consider the following program:</p><pre>(define (square x) (* x x))\n(square (id 10))</pre><p>Give the responses for <code>(square (id 10))</code> and <code>count</code> with and without memoization.</p>', code: '; Fibonacci is much slower without memoization\n; because each thunk is recomputed.\n;\n; (square (id 10)):\n; With memo: (id 10) evaluated once, count=1, result=100\n; Without memo: (id 10) evaluated twice (* x x),\n;   count=2, result=100' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.30', html: '<p>Cy D. Fect is concerned that some side effects may never take place in the lazy evaluator because of lazy evaluation of sequences. He proposes forcing every expression in a sequence except the last.</p><p>a. Ben Bitdiddle thinks Cy is wrong. Why does Ben think <code>(for-each ...)</code> works?</p><p>b. Consider Cy&rsquo;s examples with <code>(p1 1)</code> and <code>(p2 1)</code>.</p><p>c. Explain why <code>(p1 1)</code> and <code>(p2 1)</code> behave differently.</p><p>d. What approach do you prefer?</p>', code: '; a. for-each works because display is a primitive,\n;    and primitives force their arguments.\n;\n; b. (p1 1): (set! x (cons x \'(2))) uses x directly,\n;    result is (1 2). Same in both versions.\n;    (p2 1): depends on whether (set! x ...) in sequence\n;    is forced. With Cy\'s change: (1 2). Without: might\n;    leave e as a thunk.\n;\n; d. Cy\'s approach is safer for side effects.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.31', html: '<p>Implement a lazy evaluator that supports per-parameter laziness annotations: parameters marked <code>lazy</code> are delayed and memoized; parameters marked <code>lazy-memo</code> are delayed with memoization; unmarked parameters are evaluated normally.</p>', code: '; Modify procedure-parameters to return annotation info.\n; In apply, for each parameter:\n;   unmarked => (eval arg env)\n;   lazy => (delay-it arg env)\n;   lazy-memo => (delay-it-memo arg env)\n;\n; This gives fine-grained control over laziness.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.32', html: '<p>Discuss what the text&rsquo;s lazy evaluator gives us beyond what we had with the streams of chapter 3. How does making <code>cons</code> lazy differ from having explicit delay/force?</p>', code: '; With lazy cons, BOTH car and cdr are lazy.\n; Chapter 3 streams only delay the cdr.\n;\n; This means we can build data structures where\n; every part is computed on demand — true lazy\n; data structures, not just lazy sequences.\n;\n; Example: a lazy tree where branches are only\n; computed when accessed.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.33', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle tests the lazy evaluator by evaluating the expression <code>(car \'(a b c))</code> and is surprised to get an error. Explain why, and modify the evaluator to handle quoted lists correctly in the lazy language.</p>', code: '; In the lazy evaluator, cons is a non-primitive\n; procedure, so (a b c) in the quote should be\n; converted to (cons \'a (cons \'b (cons \'c \'()))).\n;\n; Modify text-of-quotation to recursively convert\n; quoted lists into cons chains:\n; (define (text-of-quotation exp env)\n;   (let ((text (cadr exp)))\n;     (if (pair? text)\n;         (eval (list \'cons (list \'quote (car text))\n;                          (list \'quote (cdr text))) env)\n;         text)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.34', html: '<p>Modify the driver loop for the lazy evaluator so that lazy pairs and lists print in some reasonable way. You may also need to modify the representation of lazy pairs so that their printed representation can be computed.</p>', code: '; Define a user-print that detects lazy pairs:\n; (define (user-print object)\n;   (cond ((compound-procedure? object)\n;          (display (list \'compound-procedure ...)))\n;         ((lazy-pair? object)\n;          (display "(") (print-lazy-list object) (display ")"))\n;         (else (display object))))\n;\n; print-lazy-list forces car and checks if cdr is\n; another lazy pair, printing elements separated by spaces.' },

  // ── Section 4.3: Nondeterministic Computing ─────────────────────────
  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.35', html: '<p>Write a procedure <code>an-integer-between</code> that returns an integer between two given bounds. This can be used to implement a procedure that finds Pythagorean triples.</p>', code: '; (define (an-integer-between low high)\n;   (require (<= low high))\n;   (amb low (an-integer-between (+ low 1) high)))\n;\n; (define (a-pythagorean-triple-between low high)\n;   (let ((i (an-integer-between low high)))\n;     (let ((j (an-integer-between i high)))\n;       (let ((k (an-integer-between j high)))\n;         (require (= (+ (* i i) (* j j)) (* k k)))\n;         (list i j k)))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.36', html: '<p>Exercise 3.69 discussed how to generate the stream of <em>all</em> Pythagorean triples, with no upper bound on the size. Explain why simply replacing <code>an-integer-between</code> with <code>an-integer-starting-from</code> in the procedure of exercise 4.35 is not an adequate way to generate arbitrary Pythagorean triples. Write a procedure that actually will accomplish this.</p>', code: '; an-integer-starting-from never exhausts, so amb\n; would try k = j, j+1, j+2, ... forever without\n; ever backtracking to try a new j or i.\n;\n; Fix: bound k based on i and j:\n; (define (pythagorean-triples)\n;   (let ((k (an-integer-starting-from 1)))\n;     (let ((j (an-integer-between 1 k)))\n;       (let ((i (an-integer-between 1 j)))\n;         (require (= (+ (* i i) (* j j)) (* k k)))\n;         (list i j k)))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.37', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle claims the following generates Pythagorean triples more efficiently:</p><pre>(define (a-pythagorean-triple-between low high)\n  (let ((i (an-integer-between low high))\n        (hsq (* high high)))\n    (let ((j (an-integer-between i high)))\n      (let ((ksq (+ (* i i) (* j j))))\n        (require (>= hsq ksq))\n        (let ((k (sqrt ksq)))\n          (require (integer? k))\n          (list i j k))))))</pre><p>Is he correct? How many possibilities are explored compared to 4.35?</p>', code: '; Yes, Ben is correct. His version doesn\'t enumerate k.\n; Instead it computes k = sqrt(i^2 + j^2) and checks\n; if it\'s an integer. This eliminates the innermost\n; amb entirely.\n;\n; 4.35 explores O(n^3) possibilities.\n; Ben\'s explores O(n^2) — much fewer.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.38', html: '<p>Modify the multiple-dwelling puzzle to omit the requirement that Fletcher does not live on either the top or the bottom floor. How many solutions are there?</p>', code: '; Remove: (require (not (= fletcher 5)))\n;         (require (not (= fletcher 1)))\n;\n; Original has 1 solution.\n; Without the Fletcher constraint, there are 5 solutions.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.39', html: '<p>Does the order of the restrictions in the multiple-dwelling procedure affect the answer? Does it affect the time to find an answer? If you think it matters, demonstrate a faster program. If you think it does not matter, argue your case.</p>', code: '; The order does NOT affect the answer (same solutions).\n; But it DOES affect performance.\n;\n; Placing the most restrictive constraints first\n; (like distinct?) prunes the search tree earlier,\n; reducing the number of possibilities explored.\n;\n; Move (require (distinct? ...)) earlier for speedup.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.40', html: '<p>In the multiple dwelling problem, how many sets of assignments are there before the restrictions are applied? Use nested <code>amb</code> with intermediate requirements to avoid unnecessary enumeration.</p>', code: '; Without restrictions: 5^5 = 3125 assignments.\n; With distinct? first: 5! = 120 permutations.\n;\n; Interleave constraints with choices:\n; (let ((baker (amb 1 2 3 4 5)))\n;   (require (not (= baker 5)))\n;   (let ((cooper (amb 1 2 3 4 5)))\n;     (require (not (= cooper 1)))\n;     (require (not (= cooper baker)))\n;     ...))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.41', html: '<p>Write an ordinary Scheme program (not using <code>amb</code>) to solve the multiple dwelling puzzle.</p>', code: '(define (multiple-dwelling)\n  (define (distinct? items)\n    (cond ((null? items) #t)\n          ((null? (cdr items)) #t)\n          ((member (car items) (cdr items)) #f)\n          (else (distinct? (cdr items)))))\n  (define (solve)\n    (let loop ((b 1) (c 1) (f 1) (m 1) (s 1) (results \'()))\n      (if (> b 5) results\n          (loop (if (< b 5) (+ b 1) 1)\n                (if (< b 5) c (if (< c 5) (+ c 1) 1))\n                (if (and (= b 5) (< c 5)) f\n                    (if (and (= b 5) (= c 5) (< f 5)) (+ f 1) 1))\n                ;; ... nested enumeration is tedious\n                ;; Better: use list comprehension approach\n                results))))\n  ; Simpler approach with filter:\n  \'use-nested-loops-or-filter)\n\n; Practical approach:\n(define (flatmap f lst) (apply append (map f lst)))\n(define (range n) (if (= n 0) \'() (append (range (- n 1)) (list n))))\n(define r5 (range 5))\n(define (distinct? items)\n  (cond ((null? items) #t) ((member (car items) (cdr items)) #f)\n        (else (distinct? (cdr items)))))\n\n(length\n (filter (lambda (x) x)\n  (flatmap (lambda (b)\n    (flatmap (lambda (c)\n      (flatmap (lambda (f)\n        (flatmap (lambda (m)\n          (map (lambda (s)\n            (if (and (distinct? (list b c f m s))\n                     (not (= b 5)) (not (= c 1)) (not (= f 1)) (not (= f 5))\n                     (> (abs (- s f)) 1) (> (abs (- f c)) 1) (not (= m 1)))\n                (list (list \'baker b) (list \'cooper c) (list \'fletcher f)\n                      (list \'miller m) (list \'smith s))\n                #f))\n            r5)) r5)) r5)) r5)) r5)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.42', html: '<p>Solve the following &ldquo;Liars&rdquo; puzzle using <code>amb</code>: Five schoolgirls each made two statements about exam rankings. Each girl told one truth and one lie. Betty: &ldquo;Kitty was second, I was third.&rdquo; Ethel: &ldquo;I was first, Joan was second.&rdquo; Joan: &ldquo;I was third, Ethel was last.&rdquo; Kitty: &ldquo;I came out second, Mary was fourth.&rdquo; Mary: &ldquo;I was fourth, Betty was first.&rdquo;</p>', code: '; Each girl: exactly one of two statements is true.\n; (define (xor a b) (and (or a b) (not (and a b))))\n;\n; (let ((betty (amb 1 2 3 4 5)) ...)\n;   (require (xor (= kitty 2) (= betty 3)))\n;   (require (xor (= ethel 1) (= joan 2)))\n;   (require (xor (= joan 3) (= ethel 5)))\n;   (require (xor (= kitty 2) (= mary 4)))\n;   (require (xor (= mary 4) (= betty 1)))\n;   (require (distinct? (list betty ethel joan kitty mary)))\n;   (list betty ethel joan kitty mary))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.43', html: '<p>Use the amb evaluator to solve the following puzzle: Five fathers each own a yacht named after one of the others\' daughters. Determine Lorna&rsquo;s father, given the clues about yacht ownership and father-daughter relationships.</p>', code: '; Five men: Moore, Barnacle, Hall, Downing, Parker\n; Moore\'s yacht: Lorna. Barnacle: Gabrielle\'s father.\n; Hall\'s yacht: Rosalind. Downing\'s yacht: Melissa.\n; Parker\'s yacht: Mary Ann. Gabrielle\'s father\'s yacht\n; is named after Parker\'s daughter.\n;\n; Use amb to assign daughters to fathers and solve.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.44', html: '<p>Exercise 2.42 described the &ldquo;eight-queens puzzle.&rdquo; Write a nondeterministic program to solve this puzzle using <code>amb</code>.</p>', code: '; (define (queens)\n;   (define (queen-in-col col)\n;     (an-integer-between 1 8))\n;   (let ((q1 (queen-in-col 1)) (q2 (queen-in-col 2))\n;         (q3 (queen-in-col 3)) (q4 (queen-in-col 4))\n;         (q5 (queen-in-col 5)) (q6 (queen-in-col 6))\n;         (q7 (queen-in-col 7)) (q8 (queen-in-col 8)))\n;     (let ((positions (list q1 q2 q3 q4 q5 q6 q7 q8)))\n;       (require (safe? positions))\n;       positions)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.45', html: '<p>With the grammar given in the text, the sentence &ldquo;The professor lectures to the student in the class with the cat&rdquo; can be parsed in five different ways. Give the five parses and explain the differences in meaning.</p>', code: '; The prepositional phrases "in the class" and\n; "with the cat" can attach at different levels:\n; 1. lectures [to student [in class [with cat]]]\n; 2. lectures [to student [in class]] [with cat]\n; 3. lectures [to student] [in class [with cat]]\n; 4. lectures [to student] [in class] [with cat]\n; 5. lectures [to [student [in class]] [with cat]]\n;\n; Each parse changes what "in the class" and\n; "with the cat" modify.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.46', html: '<p>The evaluators in sections 4.1 and 4.2 do not determine what order operands are evaluated in. We will see that the <code>amb</code> evaluator evaluates them from left to right. Explain why our parsing program wouldn&rsquo;t work if the operands were evaluated in some other order.</p>', code: '; The parser uses *unparsed* as shared state.\n; parse-word consumes from *unparsed* left to right.\n; If operands evaluated right to left, parse-verb-phrase\n; would try to parse the verb before the noun phrase,\n; consuming words out of order.\n;\n; Left-to-right order is essential because parsing\n; is inherently sequential — each parse step consumes\n; words that the next step depends on.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.47', html: '<p>Louis Reasoner suggests replacing <code>parse-verb-phrase</code> with:</p><pre>(define (parse-verb-phrase)\n  (amb (parse-word verbs)\n       (list \'verb-phrase\n             (parse-verb-phrase)\n             (parse-prepositional-phrase))))</pre><p>Does this work? Does the order of the <code>amb</code> alternatives matter?</p>', code: '; This causes infinite recursion!\n; The second alternative immediately calls\n; parse-verb-phrase again without consuming any input.\n;\n; The original version works because it first parses\n; a verb (consuming input), then optionally extends.\n;\n; Swapping the amb alternatives makes it worse:\n; infinite loop on the first alternative instead.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.48', html: '<p>Extend the grammar given above to handle more complex sentences. For example, you could add adjectives, adverbs, compound sentences with &ldquo;and&rdquo; or &ldquo;or,&rdquo; etc.</p>', code: '; Add adjectives before nouns:\n; (define (parse-simple-noun-phrase)\n;   (list \'simple-noun-phrase\n;         (parse-word articles)\n;         (parse-adjectives)   ; zero or more adjectives\n;         (parse-word nouns)))\n;\n; Add adverbs after verbs:\n; (define (parse-verb-phrase)\n;   (define (maybe-extend vp)\n;     (amb vp (maybe-extend (list \'adverb-phrase vp\n;                                (parse-word adverbs)))))\n;   (maybe-extend (parse-word verbs)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.49', html: '<p>Alyssa P. Hacker is more interested in generating sentences than in parsing them. She modifies <code>parse-word</code> to ignore the input and instead generate a word from the list. Implement Alyssa&rsquo;s idea, and show the first few sentences generated.</p>', code: '; (define (parse-word word-list)\n;   (list (car word-list)\n;         (an-element-of (cdr word-list))))\n;\n; (define (an-element-of items)\n;   (require (not (null? items)))\n;   (amb (car items) (an-element-of (cdr items))))\n;\n; Generates: "the student studies" etc.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.50', html: '<p>Implement a new special form <code>ramb</code> that is like <code>amb</code> except that it searches alternatives in a random order, rather than from left to right. Show how this can help with Alyssa&rsquo;s sentence generator.</p>', code: '; ramb shuffles alternatives before trying them.\n; This produces more varied sentences than amb,\n; which always tries the first word first.\n;\n; Implementation: in analyze-amb, randomly permute\n; the list of analyzed alternative procedures before\n; trying them.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.51', html: '<p>Implement a new kind of assignment called <code>permanent-set!</code> that is not undone upon backtracking. Use it to implement a procedure <code>count-solutions</code>.</p>', code: '; permanent-set! is like set! but not undone on failure.\n;\n; (define count 0)\n; (let ((x (an-element-of \'(a b c))))\n;   (permanent-set! count (+ count 1))\n;   (amb))\n; count  ; => 3\n;\n; Implementation: in analyze, handle permanent-set!\n; like set! but without saving the old value for\n; backtracking.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.52', html: '<p>Implement a new construct <code>if-fail</code> that takes two expressions: if the first succeeds, it returns its value; if it fails, the second expression is evaluated instead.</p>', code: '; (if-fail (let ((x (an-element-of \'(1 3 5))))\n;           (require (even? x)) x)\n;          \'all-odd)\n; => all-odd\n;\n; (if-fail (let ((x (an-element-of \'(1 3 5 8))))\n;           (require (even? x)) x)\n;          \'all-odd)\n; => 8\n;\n; Implementation: try first expression with current\n; failure continuation set to evaluate second expression.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.53', html: '<p>With <code>permanent-set!</code> and <code>if-fail</code> as described, what will be the result of evaluating the given expression that collects all Pythagorean triples?</p>', code: '; (let ((pairs \'()))\n;   (if-fail (let ((p (prime-sum-pair \'(1 3 5 8) \'(20 35 110))))\n;              (permanent-set! pairs (cons p pairs))\n;              (amb))\n;            pairs))\n;\n; Collects all prime-sum pairs into the list.\n; Result: ((8 35) (3 110) (3 20))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.54', html: '<p>If we had not realized that <code>require</code> could be implemented as an ordinary procedure using <code>amb</code>, we would have had to implement it as a special form. Implement <code>require</code> as a special form in the <code>amb</code> evaluator.</p>', code: '; (define (analyze-require exp)\n;   (let ((pproc (analyze (require-predicate exp))))\n;     (lambda (env succeed fail)\n;       (pproc env\n;              (lambda (pred-value fail2)\n;                (if pred-value\n;                    (succeed \'ok fail2)  ; or (succeed pred-value fail2)\n;                    (fail2)))\n;              fail))))\n;\n; (define (require-predicate exp) (cadr exp))' },

  // ── Section 4.4: Logic Programming ──────────────────────────────────
  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.55', html: '<p>Give simple queries that retrieve the following information from the data base:</p><p>a. all people supervised by Ben Bitdiddle;<br>b. the names and jobs of all people in the accounting division;<br>c. the names and addresses of all people who live in Slumerville.</p>', code: '; a. (supervisor ?x (Bitdiddle Ben))\n;\n; b. (job ?x (accounting . ?y))\n;\n; c. (address ?x (Slumerville . ?y))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.56', html: '<p>Formulate compound queries that retrieve:</p><p>a. the names of all people supervised by Ben Bitdiddle, together with their addresses;<br>b. all people whose salary is less than Ben Bitdiddle&rsquo;s, together with their salary and Ben&rsquo;s salary;<br>c. all people supervised by someone not in the computer division, together with the supervisor&rsquo;s name and job.</p>', code: '; a. (and (supervisor ?person (Bitdiddle Ben))\n;        (address ?person ?where))\n;\n; b. (and (salary (Bitdiddle Ben) ?ben-amount)\n;        (salary ?person ?amount)\n;        (lisp-value < ?amount ?ben-amount))\n;\n; c. (and (supervisor ?x ?boss)\n;        (not (job ?boss (computer . ?y)))\n;        (job ?boss ?boss-job))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.57', html: '<p>Define a rule that says a person can replace another person if either the first person does the same job as the second or the first can do a job the second does and the first and second are not the same person.</p>', code: '; (rule (can-replace ?p1 ?p2)\n;   (and (or (and (job ?p1 ?job) (job ?p2 ?job))\n;            (and (job ?p1 ?j1) (job ?p2 ?j2)\n;                 (can-do-job ?j1 ?j2)))\n;        (not (same ?p1 ?p2))))\n;\n; (can-replace ?x (Fect Cy D))  ; who can replace Cy?' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.58', html: '<p>Define a rule that says a person is a &ldquo;big shot&rdquo; in a division if the person works in the division but does not have a supervisor who works in the division.</p>', code: '; (rule (big-shot ?person ?division)\n;   (and (job ?person (?division . ?rest))\n;        (not (and (supervisor ?person ?boss)\n;                  (job ?boss (?division . ?rest2))))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.59', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle has set up a data base of all the weekly meetings. Define a rule for <code>meeting-time</code> that is a generalization of the data.</p><p>a. On Friday morning, Ben wants to query for all meetings that happen that day.</p><p>b. Alyssa P. Hacker asks for her Wednesday meetings. She needs a rule <code>meeting-time</code>.</p>', code: '; (rule (meeting-time ?person ?day-and-time)\n;   (or (meeting whole-company ?day-and-time)\n;       (and (job ?person (?division . ?rest))\n;            (meeting ?division ?day-and-time))))\n;\n; a. (meeting ?who (Friday ?time))\n; b. (meeting-time (Hacker Alyssa P) (Wednesday ?time))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.60', html: '<p>By giving the query <code>(lives-near ?person-1 ?person-2)</code>, Alyssa P. Hacker is able to find people who live near each other. Why does each pair appear twice? How to fix this?</p>', code: '; Each pair (A, B) matches as both\n; person-1=A, person-2=B AND person-1=B, person-2=A.\n;\n; Fix: add an ordering constraint:\n; (and (lives-near ?p1 ?p2)\n;      (lisp-value string<? \n;        (symbol->string (car ?p1))\n;        (symbol->string (car ?p2))))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.61', html: '<p>Write rules to define <code>next-to</code> in a list.</p>', code: '; (rule (?x next-to ?y in (?x ?y . ?u)))\n; (rule (?x next-to ?y in (?v . ?z))\n;   (?x next-to ?y in ?z))\n;\n; (?x next-to ?y in (1 (2 3) 4))\n; => 1 next-to (2 3), (2 3) next-to 4' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.62', html: '<p>Define rules to implement the <code>last-pair</code> operation, which returns a list containing the last element of a nonempty list.</p>', code: '; (rule (last-pair (?x) (?x)))\n; (rule (last-pair (?u . ?v) ?x)\n;   (last-pair ?v ?x))\n;\n; (last-pair (3) ?x)       => (3)\n; (last-pair (1 2 3) ?x)   => (3)\n; (last-pair (2 ?x) (3))   => ?x = 3' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.63', html: '<p>Given the biblical genealogy data base (son of Adam is Cain, etc.), define rules for <code>grandson</code> and <code>son</code> (where son can be derived from the <code>wife</code> relation).</p>', code: '; (rule (son ?m ?s)\n;   (or (son-of ?m ?s)\n;       (and (wife ?m ?w) (son-of ?w ?s))))\n;\n; (rule (grandson ?g ?s)\n;   (and (son ?g ?f) (son ?f ?s)))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.64', html: '<p>Louis Reasoner mistakenly writes <code>outranked-by</code> with the recursive call first:</p><pre>(rule (outranked-by ?staff-person ?boss)\n  (or (supervisor ?staff-person ?boss)\n      (and (outranked-by ?middle-manager ?boss)\n           (supervisor ?staff-person ?middle-manager))))</pre><p>Why does this go into an infinite loop?</p>', code: '; The recursive clause tries (outranked-by ?middle-manager ?boss)\n; BEFORE constraining ?middle-manager via supervisor.\n; This generates infinitely many unification attempts\n; with unconstrained ?middle-manager.\n;\n; The correct version constrains first:\n; (and (supervisor ?staff-person ?middle-manager)\n;      (outranked-by ?middle-manager ?boss))' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.65', html: '<p>Cy D. Fect, looking at the microshaft data base, writes: <code>(wheel ?who)</code>. He expects one result per wheel but gets duplicates. Why?</p>', code: '; The wheel rule finds ?who such that there exists\n; a chain: ?who supervises ?m who supervises ?x.\n; If ?who supervises multiple middle managers,\n; each produces a separate match.\n;\n; Oliver Warbucks supervises multiple people who\n; themselves supervise others, so he appears multiple\n; times — once per supervisory chain.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.66', html: '<p>Ben Bitdiddle realizes that queries like &ldquo;sum of all salaries&rdquo; don&rsquo;t work correctly with the query system because of duplicates. How would you deal with accumulation queries (sum, average, max) in the query language?</p>', code: '; Need to collect all distinct results, then accumulate.\n; Add an accumulation-query form:\n; (sum ?amount (and (job ?x ?j) (salary ?x ?amount)))\n;\n; Implementation: run the query, collect all bindings\n; for ?amount, remove duplicates, then sum.\n; Alternatively, use a set to track seen values.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.67', html: '<p>Devise a way to install a loop detector in the query system so as to avoid the kinds of simple loops illustrated in exercise 4.64.</p>', code: '; Maintain a history of goals being processed.\n; Before evaluating a rule body, check if the\n; current goal (after unification) is a variant of\n; a goal already in the history.\n;\n; If so, fail (return empty stream) instead of\n; recursing. This is essentially tabling/memoization\n; applied to the logic programming search.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.68', html: '<p>Define rules to implement the <code>reverse</code> operation on lists.</p>', code: '; (rule (reverse () ()))\n; (rule (reverse (?x . ?rest) ?rev)\n;   (and (reverse ?rest ?rev-rest)\n;        (append-to-form ?rev-rest (?x) ?rev)))\n;\n; (reverse (1 2 3) ?x)  => ?x = (3 2 1)\n; Note: (reverse ?x (1 2 3)) may loop in some\n; implementations.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.69', html: '<p>Define rules for <code>(great grandson)</code>, <code>(great great grandson)</code>, etc. Use the <code>ends-in-grandson</code> helper.</p>', code: '; (rule (ends-in-grandson (grandson)))\n; (rule (ends-in-grandson (?x . ?rest))\n;   (ends-in-grandson ?rest))\n;\n; (rule ((grandson) ?x ?y)\n;   (grandson ?x ?y))\n; (rule ((great . ?rel) ?x ?y)\n;   (and (ends-in-grandson ?rel)\n;        (son ?x ?z)\n;        (?rel ?z ?y)))\n;\n; ((great grandson) ?g ?ggs)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.70', html: '<p>Alyssa suggests a simpler version of <code>add-assertion!</code> that doesn&rsquo;t use <code>let</code>:</p><pre>(define (add-assertion! assertion)\n  (store-assertion-in-index assertion)\n  (set! THE-ASSERTIONS\n        (cons-stream assertion THE-ASSERTIONS)))</pre><p>This doesn&rsquo;t work. Explain why.</p>', code: '; Without the let, the cons-stream refers to\n; THE-ASSERTIONS, which by the time the stream\n; is forced, has already been set! to the NEW value.\n;\n; This creates an infinite loop: the tail of the\n; stream points back to itself.\n;\n; The let captures the OLD value before the set!.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.71', html: '<p>Louis Reasoner wonders why <code>simple-query</code> and <code>disjoin</code> use <code>delay</code>. What would happen if they were implemented without the delay?</p>', code: '; Without delay, the query system would try to compute\n; ALL results from rules before returning ANY results\n; from the database.\n;\n; With delay, database results come first (interleaved\n; with rule results), giving better interactive behavior\n; and avoiding potential infinite loops where rules\n; generate unbounded results.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.72', html: '<p>Why do <code>disjoin</code> and <code>stream-flatmap</code> use <code>interleave</code> instead of <code>stream-append</code>? Construct an example where <code>stream-append</code> would hang.</p>', code: '; If one branch produces an infinite stream,\n; stream-append would never get to the second branch.\n;\n; Example: (or (always-true) (job ?x ?y))\n; With stream-append: always-true generates infinite\n; results, job query never reached.\n; With interleave: alternates between both branches.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.73', html: '<p>Why does <code>flatten-stream</code> use <code>delay</code>? What would happen without it?</p>', code: '; flatten-stream processes a stream of streams.\n; Without delay, it would try to fully flatten the\n; first sub-stream before moving to the next.\n;\n; With delay (via interleave-delayed), it interleaves\n; results from different sub-streams, ensuring\n; fairness and avoiding hangs on infinite sub-streams.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.74', html: '<p>Alyssa P. Hacker proposes using <code>stream-flatmap</code> implemented with <code>simple-flatten</code> instead of the interleaving version, since the individual streams being combined are not infinite in the cases where <code>not</code> and <code>lisp-value</code> are used.</p><pre>(define (simple-flatten stream)\n  (stream-map stream-car\n              (stream-filter (lambda (s) (not (stream-null? s))) stream)))</pre><p>Does this work? Does it change behavior?</p>', code: '; This works correctly for the cases where each\n; sub-stream has at most one element (which is true\n; for simple queries against the database).\n;\n; But if a frame produces multiple results from a\n; query, simple-flatten only takes the first from\n; each sub-stream, losing results. So it\'s not\n; equivalent in the general case.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.75', html: '<p>Implement the <code>unique</code> special form for the query language. <code>(unique (job ?x (computer wizard)))</code> should succeed if exactly one person has the job title <code>(computer wizard)</code>.</p>', code: '; (define (uniquely-asserted operands frame-stream)\n;   (stream-flatmap\n;    (lambda (frame)\n;      (let ((results (qeval (unique-query operands)\n;                           (singleton-stream frame))))\n;        (if (and (not (stream-null? results))\n;                 (stream-null? (stream-cdr results)))\n;            results\n;            the-empty-stream)))\n;    frame-stream))\n;\n; (put \'unique \'qeval uniquely-asserted)' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.76', html: '<p>Our implementation of <code>and</code> as a series of queries is not efficient. Devise an implementation of <code>and</code> that processes the two clauses independently, then merges compatible frames.</p>', code: '; (define (conjoin-independent q1 q2 frame-stream)\n;   (stream-flatmap\n;    (lambda (f1)\n;      (stream-filter\n;       (lambda (merged) merged)  ; keep non-#f\n;       (stream-map\n;        (lambda (f2) (merge-frames f1 f2))\n;        (qeval q2 (singleton-stream (make-empty-frame))))))\n;    (qeval q1 frame-stream)))\n;\n; merge-frames unifies bindings; returns #f on conflict.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.77', html: '<p>The <code>not</code> and <code>lisp-value</code> filters must be delayed until enough variables are bound. Devise a way to handle this automatically.</p>', code: '; Attach filters to frames as "pending" constraints.\n; When a variable gets bound (during unification),\n; check if any pending filter now has all its\n; variables bound. If so, apply the filter.\n;\n; Each frame carries a list of delayed filters.\n; After each unification step, scan pending filters\n; and execute those that are ready.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.78', html: '<p>Redesign the query language as a nondeterministic program to be implemented using the <code>amb</code> evaluator, rather than as a stream process.</p>', code: '; Replace stream operations with amb:\n; (define (query pattern)\n;   (let ((assertion (an-element-of assertions)))\n;     (let ((result (unify pattern assertion \'())))\n;       (require result)\n;       result)))\n;\n; Rules become: try each rule with amb,\n; unify the conclusion, then recursively query\n; the rule body. Backtracking handles search.' },

  { t: 'exercise', n: '4.79', html: '<p>When we implemented the Prolog-like logic language, we used a data structure called a <em>frame</em> to represent partial answers. Devise a way to handle the scoping of rule variables correctly, considering that rule application can be nested and rule variables should not interfere with each other.</p>', code: '; Current approach: rename variables in each rule\n; application with unique suffixes (e.g., ?x-1, ?x-2).\n;\n; Alternative: use lexical scoping for rules.\n; Each rule application creates a new "scope" with\n; fresh variable bindings, similar to how procedure\n; application creates a new environment frame.\n;\n; This is essentially what the renaming achieves,\n; but framing it as scoping makes it clearer.' },

];
